{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>This is the thing.</b> The verse's saying <b>זֶה הַדָּבָר</b> may be explained in light of what they said in tractate Bava Batra (120a), and this is their language: \"The daughters of Tzelofchad were permitted to marry whomever they wished, as it says: לַטּוֹב בְּעֵינֵיהֶם — rather, how do I fulfill אַךְ לְמִשְׁפַּחַת אֲבִיהֶן וְגוֹ׳? — good advice,\" etc., end quote. And this is the verse's saying <b>זֶה הַדָּבָר</b> — meaning: because He first said ״כֵּן מַטֵּה בְנֵי יוֹסֵף דּוֹבְרִים״ — that the law is with them — and then went back and explained that this law concerning the daughters of Tzelofchad was not included within the parameters of this legal decree; and that is His saying <b>זֶה הַדָּבָר אֲשֶׁר צִוָּה ה׳ לִבְנוֹת צְלָפְחָד</b> — that the permission is in their hand to do what is good in their eyes, etc. And He was precise in saying ״זֶה״ to say 'this' and not another — that HaShem did not grant this distinction through this permission except to them alone; and this is what He alluded to by saying <b>בְּנוֹת צְלָפְחָד לֵאמֹר</b>, for the word לֵאמֹר has no [ordinary] meaning [here], and according to our way [of interpretation] it intends to say that the God came in this utterance to exalt their honor."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 36, :register {:flags {0 ["God"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:17:22.520185983Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}